\chapter{Traversal, Dumping, and Tree Utilities}
\label{ch:traversal}

\section{There is no traversal helper, and that is deliberate}

Every algorithm that consumes an abstract syntax tree walks it. A
pretty-printer walks it to produce text; an evaluator walks it to produce a
value; a linter walks it to find problems; a rewriter walks it to transform
it. It would be reasonable to expect \DSLtk{} to ship a walker alongside
the node type.

It does not. \api{ASTNode}, as described in \chapref{ch:astnode}, exposes
no visitor, no \code{walk} member, no iterator, and no fold. What it does
expose is \code{children()}, and the header itself uses it the way a user
would: at line 6860 the rewrite engine iterates
\code{for (auto &child : n.children ())}, at line 9339 the S-expression
printer iterates \code{for (const auto &child : node.children ())}, and at
line 10001 the visitor's schema collector does the same. The pattern is the
API.

The reason for the omission is a design position rather than an
oversight. \api{ASTNode} is a value type with no type-level tag, as
\chapref{ch:leaf-and-node} established, so a walker cannot dispatch on the
node's kind at compile time. A generic walker could still be written --- it
would take a callback --- and \chapref{ch:visitors} eventually provides
something along those lines. But the toolkit declines to guess at what a
caller wants to do at each node, and it declines to impose a traversal
order. The consequence for the reader is that traversal is a dozen lines of
user code, and that the dozen lines are visible, adjustable, and cheap to
reason about.

\section{A worked example}

The program below implements the three traversals this chapter discusses: a
node count, a tag search, and an in-place rewrite. It also shows a
tag-dispatch loop built on the compile-time dispatch of
\chapref{ch:pattern-match}.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Three hand-written traversals of an \api{ASTNode} tree, plus a tag dispatch.},label={lst:traversal-demo}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <iostream>
#include <optional>
#include <string>
#include <string_view>

using dsl::ASTNode;

// 1. How many nodes does the tree contain?
std::size_t
count_nodes (ASTNode const &n)
{
  std::size_t total = 1;
  for (auto const &child : n.children ())
    total += count_nodes (child);
  return total;
}

// 2. The first node carrying a given tag, or null.
ASTNode const *
find_by_tag (ASTNode const &n, std::string_view tag)
{
  if (n.tag () == tag)
    return &n;
  for (auto const &child : n.children ())
    if (auto const *hit = find_by_tag (child, tag))
      return hit;
  return nullptr;
}

// 3. Constant folding for binary "add" over numeric literals.
std::optional<ASTNode>
fold_add (ASTNode const &n)
{
  if (n.is_leaf () || n.tag () != "add" || n.size () != 2)
    return std::nullopt;
  if (!n.child (0).is_leaf () || n.child (0).tag () != "num")
    return std::nullopt;
  if (!n.child (1).is_leaf () || n.child (1).tag () != "num")
    return std::nullopt;
  int a = std::stoi (n.child (0).value ());
  int b = std::stoi (n.child (1).value ());
  return dsl::leaf<"num"> (a + b);
}

// 4. Bottom-up rewrite that mutates the tree in place.
void
rewrite_in_place (ASTNode &n)
{
  for (auto &child : n.children ())
    rewrite_in_place (child);
  if (auto replacement = fold_add (n))
    n = std::move (*replacement);
}

// 5. Tag dispatch: map the runtime tag onto an enum, then match on it.
enum class Op
{
  Add,
  Num,
  Other
};

Op
classify (std::string_view tag)
{
  if (tag == "add")
    return Op::Add;
  if (tag == "num")
    return Op::Num;
  return Op::Other;
}

int
main ()
{
  ASTNode tree = dsl::node<"add"> (dsl::node<"add"> (dsl::leaf<"num"> (2),
                                                     dsl::leaf<"num"> (3)),
                                   dsl::leaf<"num"> (4));

  std::cout << "tree     : " << tree.dump () << '\n';
  std::cout << "nodes    : " << count_nodes (tree) << '\n';

  if (auto const *hit = find_by_tag (tree, "num"))
    std::cout << "first num: " << hit->value () << '\n';

  ASTNode copy = tree;
  rewrite_in_place (copy);
  std::cout << "folded   : " << copy.dump () << '\n';
  std::cout << "untouched: " << tree.dump () << '\n';

  // Tag dispatch through dsl::match.
  auto handler = dsl::match (
      dsl::when<Op::Add> (
          [] (Op, ASTNode const &n) { return "add with " + std::to_string (n.size ()) + " children"; }),
      dsl::when<Op::Num> (
          [] (Op, ASTNode const &n) { return "num = " + n.value (); }),
      dsl::otherwise (
          [] (Op, ASTNode const &n) { return "unhandled tag " + std::string (n.tag ()); }));

  for (auto const &child : tree.children ())
    std::cout << "dispatch : " << handler (classify (child.tag ()), child) << '\n';

  // dump() does not quote: a value with a space is ambiguous.
  ASTNode spaced = dsl::leaf<"str"> ("hello world");
  std::cout << "unquoted : " << spaced.dump () << '\n';
  return 0;
}
\end{lstlisting}

The output is

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:traversal-demo}.},label={lst:traversal-demo-out}]
tree     : (add (add (num 2) (num 3)) (num 4))
nodes    : 5
first num: 2
folded   : (num 9)
untouched: (add (add (num 2) (num 3)) (num 4))
dispatch : add with 2 children
dispatch : num = 4
unquoted : (str hello world)
\end{lstlisting}

\section{The four shapes of traversal}

