\subsection{Manual recursion}

The simplest traversal is the one the header writes. A function takes a
node, does something with it, and recurses over \code{children()}:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={A manual recursive walk.},label={lst:manual-recursion}]
void
for_each (dsl::ASTNode const &n, std::function<void (dsl::ASTNode const &)> f)
{
  f (n);
  for (auto const &child : n.children ())
    for_each (child, f);
}
\end{lstlisting}

The order is determined entirely by where the call to \code{f} sits. Put it
before the loop and the walk is pre-order; put it after the loop and the
walk is post-order. Moving a single line changes the semantics of the whole
program, which is both the strength and the hazard of the manual style.

\subsection{A callback walk}

The manual recursion above becomes a callback walk when the recursion is
hidden behind a named function. A general-purpose walker looks like this:

\begin{pseudocode}[A generic tree walk]
\textbf{function} walk (n, pre, post)\\
\quad \textbf{if} pre is not empty \textbf{then} pre (n)\\
\quad \textbf{for} c \textbf{in} n.children \textbf{do}\\
\qquad walk (c, pre, post)\\
\quad \textbf{if} post is not empty \textbf{then} post (n)
\end{pseudocode}

This is the shape the toolkit's own rewrite engine uses internally. The
recursive helper \api{rewrite_once} of \chapref{ch:rewrite-sets} walks the
children first, then acts on the node --- a post-order walk with a single
action, but with the action fused into the recursion.

\subsection{Tag dispatch with \code{dsl::match}}

When the action at each node depends on the node's tag, the natural
tool is the compile-time dispatch table of \chapref{ch:pattern-match}:
\code{dsl::match}, \code{dsl::when}, and \code{dsl::otherwise}. There is,
however, a genuine obstacle, and it is worth stating precisely because the
header's own documentation suggests otherwise.

\code{dsl::when} takes its key as a non-type template parameter of type
\code{auto}. Such a parameter must be a \emph{structural} type: an
integral, an enumeration, a pointer to an object with static storage
duration, or a literal class type whose members are all structural. Two
consequences follow, and both are visible in the shipped header.

First, a string literal cannot be a key. The compiler says so directly:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={A string literal cannot be a \code{when} key.},label={lst:when-string}]
auto t = dsl::match (
    dsl::when<"add"> ([] (std::string_view k) { return "add"; }),
    dsl::otherwise ([] (std::string_view k) { return "other"; }));
// error: '"add"' is not a valid template argument for type 'const char*'
// note: string literals can never be used in this context
\end{lstlisting}

Second, the key must be a value, not a type name. The header's documentation
at lines 6173, 6300 and 6347 omits the object construction after the pattern
type. Both \code{FixedString} and \code{pattern} satisfy the relevant
structural-type requirements; a user-provided constexpr constructor does not
disqualify a class. Construct a pattern object with \texttt{\{\}} to use it
as the key.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={A structural pattern object is a valid dispatch key.},label={lst:when-pattern}]
auto t = dsl::match (
    dsl::when<dsl::pattern<"add">{}> ([] (std::string_view k) { return "add"; }),
    dsl::otherwise ([] (std::string_view k) { return "other"; }));
// t(std::string_view("add")) returns "add".
\end{lstlisting}

Another option is to map the runtime tag onto an enumeration and dispatch
on the enumeration, exactly as \code{classify} does in
Listing~\ref{lst:traversal-demo}. An enumeration \emph{is} a structural
type, so \code{dsl::when<Op::Add>(...)} compiles, and the
\code{std::equality_comparable_with} test inside \api{WhenClause} succeeds
because the key type and the clause key type are the same enumeration. The
price is one comparison chain per node, and the requirement that the
enumeration be kept in step with the tags the tree actually uses.

\begin{warnbox}[The documented \code{when<pattern<...>>} form does not compile]
The header's documentation for \code{dsl::pattern} and \code{dsl::when}
shows \code{dsl::when<dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">>(handler)} as the
pattern-based dispatch form. In the shipped code that expression is
ill-formed because it supplies a type where an \code{auto} value is required.
The working form is \code{dsl::when<dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">{}>(handler)}.
The \code{try_invoke} member recognizes the pattern's static \code{matches}
operation. Chapter 10 and the companion pattern probe exercise this path.
\end{warnbox}

\subsection{\code{ASTVisitor}: the batteries-included alternative}

\chapref{ch:visitors} describes the \api{ASTVisitor} of \texttt{DSLtk.hpp}
at line 9992, which is the toolkit's own answer to the traversal problem.
It is worth contrasting it with the hand-written walks here.

An \api{ASTVisitor} can be built from a schema tree --- a sample tree whose
tags are runtime strings --- and a set of cases, each
pairing a tag with a handler. Construction walks the schema, collects the
distinct tags, and throws \code{std::invalid_argument} if any tag has no
handler. The visitor then offers \api{visit}, which invokes the handler
for one node, and \api{walk}, which visits the whole tree in either
pre-order or post-order.

Three differences matter in practice. The visitor validates that every tag
in the schema is handled, so a missing handler is caught at construction
time rather than at the first node that happens to carry the missing tag.
The visitor dispatches by comparing the node's runtime tag against each
case's compile-time tag, which is a string comparison per node --- the same
work the enumeration mapping does, but without the user having to maintain
an enumeration. And the visitor's \api{walk} is a fixed recursion, so it
inherits the depth limitation discussed below.

The compile-time schema machinery goes further: \api{compile_visitor}
takes a schema \emph{type} rather than a schema tree, and a
\code{static_assert} demands that the cases cover every tag in the
schema, recursively. That is the closest thing \DSLtk{} has to a
compile-time guarantee about an AST's shape, and it exists precisely
because \api{ASTNode} cannot carry its tag in its type.

\section{Dumping, comparing, and the limits of both}

\subsection{\code{dump()} as a debugging primitive}

\api{dump()} was described in \chapref{ch:astnode}: a recursive
S-expression-like rendering that writes \code{"(tag value)"} for a leaf
with a value, \code{"(tag)"} for a leaf without one, and
\code{"(tag child1 child2 ...)"} for an inner node. It is the right tool
for a diagnostic message and the wrong tool for a wire format, for one
reason: it does not quote.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Unquoted values are ambiguous.},label={lst:unquoted}]
(str hello world)
\end{lstlisting}

The rendering above cannot be parsed back into a two-element tree, because
the parser cannot tell whether \code{hello} and \code{world} are one value
or two. The same applies to a value containing a parenthesis, which would
close the node early. A DSL whose leaf values may contain arbitrary text
must either quote them itself before storing them, or use the \api{SExpr}
machinery of \chapref{ch:sexpressions}, which has a documented escaping
rule.

The second limitation of \api{dump()} is that it is lossy in the other
direction as well: an inner node with no children and a leaf with an empty
value both render as \code{"(tag)"}, and the two are not equal nodes. A
round trip through \api{dump()} therefore loses the leaf/inner distinction
for empty nodes.

\subsection{Structural comparison}

\api{operator==} compares two trees structurally, as
\chapref{ch:astnode} described: the tags must match, the leaf-ness must
match, and then either the values or the children vectors must match. For
a rewriting system this is exactly the right notion of equality, and it is
the notion the rewrite engine uses to decide whether a pass changed
anything --- indirectly, through the \code{changed} flag that
\api{rewrite_once} maintains.

There is no explicitly declared \code{operator!=}, but C++20 rewrites
\code{a != b} using the Boolean equality operator. There is no \code{operator<} and no
\code{std::hash} specialisation, so an \api{ASTNode} cannot be sorted and
cannot be a key in an \code{unordered_container}. A user who wants to
deduplicate trees must write a hash function, which means walking the tree
and combining the hash of every tag and value --- a piece of work the
toolkit does not do for them.

\subsection{Why \code{child(i)} throws}

\api{child(i)} is defined in terms of \code{children_.at(i)}, which throws
\code{std::out_of_range} when the index is out of range. The alternative
would have been \code{operator[]}, which is unchecked.

For a tree library this is the right choice. A traversal that indexes
children without checking is a traversal that will eventually read past
the end of a vector, and the failure --- a corrupted program, or a crash
somewhere far from the cause --- is far harder to diagnose than an
exception with a message naming the offending index. The cost is that
every predicate which assumes a particular arity must check it, and a
predicate that forgets will throw out of the middle of a rewrite pass
rather than producing a wrong answer.

\begin{tipbox}[Write arity checks into the predicate]
A predicate that inspects \code{n.child(0)} should begin with a test of
\code{n.size()}. A predicate that inspects a child's tag should also test
\code{n.child(0).is_leaf()}, because a child may be an inner node whose
tag happens to be the same string. These two checks, written once in a
helper, remove almost every way a rule can crash.
\end{tipbox}

\section{Mutation, depth, and the shape of a rewrite}

\subsection{\code{children()} hands out a mutable reference}

The const and mutable overloads of \code{children()} return the same
underlying vector; the mutable one simply drops the \code{const}. This is
the hinge between traversal and rewriting. A traversal written as
\code{for (auto &child : n.children ())} can modify the tree it is
walking, and \code{rewrite_in_place} in Listing~\ref{lst:traversal-demo}
does exactly that: it folds constants by replacing the node in place with
a freshly built leaf.

Two properties of this style are worth spelling out. First, it is cheap:
the in-place rewrite allocates one new node per folded constant and nothing
else, whereas the copy-based engine of \chapref{ch:rewrite-sets} allocates
a new node for every node it visits. Second, it is limited: as
\chapref{ch:astnode} established, there is no mutable accessor for a
node's tag or for a leaf's value, so an in-place rewrite can only change a
node's children or replace the node wholesale. A rule that wants to
retag a node must build a replacement and assign it --- which is what
\code{n = std::move (*replacement)} does.

\subsection{Recursion depth}

Every traversal in this chapter is recursive, and so is
\api{rewrite_once}, and so is \api{dump()}, and so is the destructor of
\api{ASTNode} when it destroys a \code{std::vector<ASTNode>}. There is no
tail-call guarantee in C++ and no explicit stack in any of these
functions, so the depth of the tree bounds the depth of the C++ stack
frame chain.

For a hand-written source language, trees are usually shallow: an
expression nest of a few dozen levels is already unusual, and the default
stack of a POSIX thread is typically eight megabytes, which buys
thousands of frames. For a tree built by a machine --- a parsed document,
a generated term, a deserialised graph --- the depth can be arbitrary, and
a traversal of a ten-thousand-deep tree will exhaust the stack. The
toolkit offers no iterative traversal, no depth limit, and no diagnostic.

\begin{warnbox}[Depth is the practical limit]
If a tree can be arbitrarily deep, do not walk it with recursion. Either
bound the depth when the tree is built, or maintain an explicit stack of
pending nodes. The rewrite engine of \chapref{ch:rewrite-sets} has the same
exposure, and its \code{max_iterations} bound does not help: it bounds
the number of passes, not the depth of a single pass.
\end{warnbox}

\section{The traversal-shaped utilities}

Two other parts of the toolkit are traversals in disguise, and it is useful
to see them in this light.

\api{ast_to_sexpr} (line 9332) and \api{sexpr_to_ast} (line 9343) of
\chapref{ch:sexpressions} walk a tree and build a tree respectively. The
first is a post-order traversal that produces an \api{SExpr} list whose
head is the node's tag; the second is a pre-order traversal of an
\api{SExpr} that produces an \api{ASTNode}. Neither uses the factories of
\chapref{ch:leaf-and-node}, because the tags they handle are runtime
strings.

The round trip between them is lossy in an instructive way.
\code{ast_to_sexpr} emits \code{(tag value)} for a leaf and
\code{(tag child ...)} for an inner node; \code{sexpr_to_ast} reads a
two-element list whose second element is an atom as a \emph{leaf}. So an
inner node with exactly one child whose child is an atom is read back as a
leaf, and a bare atom is read back with the tag \code{"atom"}. The
conversion is convenient for interchange between the two subsystems; it is
not an identity.

The e-graph subsystem of \chapref{ch:egraphs} is a third traversal-shaped
utility, but of a different kind: it never walks an \api{ASTNode}. It
hash-conses its own nodes, maintains equivalence classes with a union-find,
and rebuilds congruence after every round of rule applications. Its
\code{extract} function then reconstructs a finite tree, and its
\code{extract_ast} function converts that tree into an \api{ASTNode} using
the \api{ASTNode} constructors. The comparison with the rewrite engine of
\chapref{ch:rewrite-sets} is deferred to that chapter and to
\chapref{ch:egraphs}; the point here is only that the two subsystems
produce the same output type by completely different means.

\section{Edge cases and limits}

\paragraph{No built-in traversal of any kind.}
There is no \code{walk}, no \code{visit}, no \code{for_each_child}, and
no range adaptor. Every traversal is user code. The cost is a dozen lines
per project; the benefit is that the traversal is exactly what the
application needs.

\paragraph{No iteration order is prescribed.}
Because \code{children()} returns a \code{std::vector}, the order of the
children is the order in which they were inserted, and it is stable. But
no algorithm in the toolkit promises that a rule will be applied in
child order, and the rewrite engine's bottom-up pass visits children
before parents without promising anything about siblings.

\paragraph{No depth limit and no diagnostic.}
Nothing counts recursion depth. A deep tree is a stack overflow, and the
failure carries no indication of which traversal caused it.

\paragraph{No protection against concurrent mutation.}
\code{children()} hands out a mutable reference into the node's internals.
Two threads that mutate the same tree concurrently are in undefined
behaviour territory; concurrent readers are safe.

\paragraph{\code{dump()} is not invertible.}
As described above, \code{dump()} neither quotes nor distinguishes an
empty inner node from an empty leaf. A tree printed and read back is not
necessarily the tree that was printed.

\paragraph{No hashing, no ordering, no inequality.}
The comparison surface of \api{ASTNode} is exactly one operator. Any
algorithm that needs a set, a map, or a sort key over trees must supply
its own.

\paragraph{Tag dispatch needs a hand-written mapping.}
Because \code{dsl::when} cannot take a string key, a user who wants
compile-time dispatch on tags must maintain an enumeration and a mapping
function by hand, and must keep them in step with the tags the tree
actually uses. The \api{ASTVisitor} of \chapref{ch:visitors} removes that
bookkeeping at the price of requiring a schema.

\paragraph{The in-place style has a smaller repertoire.}
An in-place rewrite can restructure a tree but cannot retag a node or
change a leaf's text without replacing the node. Rules that need to do
either must allocate, and once one allocates, the copy-based engine of
\chapref{ch:rewrite-sets} is usually the simpler choice.

\section{Closing}

Traversal in \DSLtk{} is what it is everywhere else in the toolkit: a small
primitive set, and a composition of those primitives by the user. The
range over \code{children()} is the whole vocabulary; the four shapes in
\tabref{tab:traversal-shapes} are four ways of using it. The two things a
reader should carry forward are that \code{children()} is a mutable
reference, which makes in-place rewriting possible, and that the toolkit
does not prescribe an order, which makes the order the reader's to choose
--- and to pay for. \chapref{ch:rewrite} takes these observations and turns
them into rules.
